# podkit — machine-specific configuration.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` and edit. `.env.local` is gitignored and the runtime
# loads it automatically — from the REPO ROOT, whatever the working directory
# is, because most entry points here run from a package directory (see
# test-packages/substrate/src/env-file.ts). A variable already exported into
# the environment always beats the file. This file is committed and must never
# contain a real value. Nothing here is needed to run the unit, integration or
# e2e suites — it configures the device *substrate*, which only `test:vm` and
# the harness scripts use.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs here, and what does not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Here:     WHICH substrate this machine drives, and the credentials that let
#           it lifecycle one.
# Not here: HOW to reach a substrate. Hostname, user, SSH key and any jump host
#           live in your own `~/.ssh/config` under the alias the registry names
#           (`sshAlias` in test-packages/substrate/src/registry.ts). The repo
#           declares capability; your machine declares connection. That split is
#           what keeps a public repository free of anyone's infrastructure by
#           construction rather than by vigilance — see ADR-029 §2.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Substrate selection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which substrate the device harness runs on, by registry id.
#
#   device        the local Lima VM (requires `limactl` on PATH)
#   deviceRemote  an SSH-reachable Debian box, resolved through the
#                 `podkit-substrate` alias in your ~/.ssh/config
#
# Only *device* substrates are selectable here. The builder is a separate role
# with its own variable below — a box that compiles artifacts is disqualified
# from running them, since the toolchain it carries is exactly what a substrate
# must not have. See docs/environments/builder-proxmox.md.
#
# This variable does influence the build host, though, and deliberately: with
# nothing else set, artifacts are built on the builder provisioned the same way
# as the substrate that will run them.
#
# Leave it unset and selection falls back to the Lima substrate when `limactl`
# is present, ANNOUNCING that it did so, and errors otherwise. The announcement
# matters: if you have a remote substrate configured and forget to select it,
# a silent fallback would hand you a local result that looks like a remote one.
#
# Platform is never consulted. A Mac keeps working with nothing set because
# Lima is installed on it, not because anything special-cases macOS.
#PODKIT_SUBSTRATE=device


# Architecture artifacts are built FOR. Normally leave this unset.
#
# It is resolved from the selected substrate — the box a binary has to start on
# decides what the binary must be — in two ways, neither of which you configure.
# An ssh substrate DECLARES its architecture in the registry, so selecting one
# is already enough: `bun run test:vm` on an arm64 Mac with PODKIT_SUBSTRATE
# pointing at an amd64 box builds amd64, announcing that it did. Commands that
# hold a link measure it instead (`harness:install` asks for `uname -m`). With
# no substrate at all it falls back to this host's own architecture, which is
# why a single-machine setup never has to think about it.
#
# Note what this value does NOT decide: how many architectures a run builds.
# Two surfaces want musl binaries for DIFFERENT machines — `test:e2e:docker-
# dist` runs inside the substrate and wants the substrate's architecture, while
# `test:e2e:docker-loopback` builds an image on THIS machine's Docker and wants
# the host's — and they are siblings in one turbo invocation. So the musl build
# produces every architecture the run needs rather than the one it targets: one
# set when host and substrate agree, both sets when they differ, each compiled
# on a build host that can actually produce it. `quality` therefore covers both
# cells in a single pass on a cross-architecture setup, and a same-architecture
# setup starts nothing it did not start before. The glibc artifacts are
# untouched, because every one of their consumers is inside the substrate.
#
# The companion variable `PODKIT_HOST_ARCH` is stamped automatically by the
# turbo wrapper and is not yours to set: it puts this machine's architecture in
# the cache key of the musl tasks, whose outputs depend on it.
#
# Set it only to build for a machine that is not reachable right now. It then
# wins over the substrate, and the disagreement is caught at the point it costs
# something: the transfer refuses to install an artifact whose ELF header does
# not match the substrate's `uname -m`.
#
# Accepts `arm64`/`aarch64` and `x64`/`x86_64`/`amd64`, so `$(uname -m)` is
# always a correct value.
#PODKIT_TARGET_ARCH=x86_64


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build-host selection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# WHICH box compiles this machine's Linux artifacts, by registry id.
#
#   builderGlibc   the local Lima glibc builder VM   (requires `limactl`)
#   builderMusl    the local Lima musl builder VM    (requires `limactl`)
#   builderRemote  an SSH-reachable Debian box, resolved through the
#                  `podkit-builder` alias in your ~/.ssh/config; it serves musl
#                  from an Alpine container rather than from a second guest
#
# Normally leave it unset. The rules pick correctly for both setups that exist:
# a build host must be able to produce the `(architecture, libc)` the run needs,
# and among those that can, the one provisioned like the selected substrate
# wins. An arm64 Mac driving an amd64 substrate therefore reaches for the remote
# builder on its own, because no local Lima VM can produce amd64 at all.
#
# Set it to pin a specific box — a second amd64 machine you own, or a builder
# that is not the substrate's sibling. A configured value that cannot produce
# the target is refused rather than honoured: building anyway would write the
# wrong bytes under the right filename.
#PODKIT_BUILD_HOST=builderRemote

# "Build on THIS machine" is the `podkit-builder` alias pointed at localhost.
#
# There is deliberately no third provisioner for it. A build host is reached
# over a link, an `ssh` entry names how it is reached and nothing about where it
# is, and a `Host podkit-builder` stanza with `HostName localhost` is a complete
# answer — same code path, same contract, no in-process special case to keep
# correct. The machine still has to pass `builder-doctor.sh`; a dev box with a
# partial toolchain is not a builder just because it is the one you are sitting
# at.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proxmox VE — substrate lifecycle (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only needed to CREATE, start, stop, snapshot or destroy a Proxmox-hosted
# substrate from this repo. Everything else — the doctor, binary install, and
# every test suite — works over the SSH link with none of this set; the
# lifecycle verbs then print the equivalent `qm` command for you to run by hand.
#
# The token is privilege-separated and ACL'd to a dedicated pool, so its
# effective rights are the intersection of its user's and its own. Create it
# with the `pveum` recipe in docs/environments/ — never by handing a token the
# root account's privileges.

# The token, its secret and the fingerprint are produced by phase 1 — see
# docs/environments/device-substrate-proxmox.md step 1, or run
# `bootstrap-pve.sh --print-only` to see what it does first.
#
# The split they exist to serve: ONE privileged bootstrap on the PVE host
# creates the pool, the token, the cloud-init snippets and the pinned image.
# After that a machine holding only these three values can create, start, stop,
# destroy and recreate guests in that pool and nothing else — and a machine
# holding only an ssh key can apply the contract, install binaries and run every
# suite, with no PVE rights at all.

# Base URL of the PVE API, including the scheme and port.
#
# A full URL rather than a hostname so a non-default port or a reverse proxy
# needs no second key. Not an ssh_config alias like the guests use: an alias is
# resolved by a file that has no bearing on an HTTPS connection, so it would
# look like configuration while doing nothing.
#PODKIT_PVE_API_URL=https://pve.example:8006

# Token id, in PVE's `user@realm!tokenname` form.
#PODKIT_PVE_TOKEN_ID=podkit@pve!automation

# Token secret — the UUID PVE prints ONCE at creation. A secret, in a gitignored
# file, on one machine. If it ever reaches a commit, revoke it rather than
# rewriting history.
#PODKIT_PVE_TOKEN_SECRET=

# SHA-256 fingerprint of the PVE host's TLS certificate, colon-separated, as
# `openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256` prints it.
#
# There is no flag to turn TLS verification off. A PVE host presents only its
# leaf, signed by a cluster CA that never reaches the wire, so ordinary chain
# validation cannot succeed — the pin REPLACES it, checked on the socket before
# any request is sent. That trusts exactly this one certificate, where a blanket
# insecure switch would trust anything, permanently, on every connection.
#
# Leave it unset only if your API sits behind a certificate a public CA signed;
# system-CA validation then applies as normal.
#PODKIT_PVE_TLS_FINGERPRINT=


# The pool the token's ACL is confined to, and the storages and bridge the
# guests use. These must match what you passed to `bootstrap-pve.sh`; the
# defaults are the ones the playbook documents.
#
# PODKIT_PVE_STORAGE is positional and is a LIST: disk storage first, the
# storage holding the cloud-init snippet and the pinned image second. Naming
# only one is the most likely way to get this wrong — a token granted a single
# storage 403s on Datastore.Audit for the other, which surfaces much later as a
# `--cicustom` that resolves to nothing.
#PODKIT_PVE_POOL=podkit
#PODKIT_PVE_STORAGE=local-lvm local
#PODKIT_PVE_BRIDGE=vmbr0

# There is deliberately no key naming the PVE NODE. `GET /pools/<pool>` already
# returns the node for every member, so a second place to say it could only
# ever agree or be wrong.


# VMIDs of the guests the lifecycle verbs act on.
#
# Here rather than in the registry for the same reason the ssh alias is: PVE
# addresses a guest by VMID, and a VMID is a fact about your hypervisor, not
# about podkit. The repo declares the ROLE (`deviceRemote`, `builderRemote` in
# test-packages/substrate/src/registry.ts); your machine declares which guest
# fills it.
#PODKIT_PVE_VMID_DEVICE_REMOTE=9000
#PODKIT_PVE_VMID_BUILDER_REMOTE=9001
